=== buried_ending ===

= at_the_portal

The carvings here are older than the ones above. Older than the mine, older than the corridors, older than any tool that cut them. They cover the chamber walls floor to ceiling: concentric arcs, angular intersections, each symbol interlocking with the next in a continuous circuit. The containment pattern. The original. Not fragments, not copies, not the traced maintenance lines in the upper tunnels. The source.

Most of it is intact. The lines are deep, cut into the natural stone by hands that understood what they were holding shut. Whoever carved this did it completely, precisely, with the patience of someone who knew what would happen if they left a gap.

They left no gaps. The mine did.

Three fractures. Hairline cracks where the stone shifted in 1973, when the main shaft punched through from above, when the drill bit crossed into something the sounding equipment couldn't measure and forty-eight men fell into a space that wasn't a collapse. The cracks are thin. A few inches each. But a few inches is enough. A circuit with a break in it is not a circuit.

{bex_relationship >= 5: One of the fractures has been recently worked. The groove is deeper than the surrounding stone, the edges less worn. Someone traced it with a tool, carefully, deliberately, filling the crack partway. Bex's hand. You recognize the work the way you recognize a musician's phrasing. They got this close. Close enough to start. Not close enough to finish. The proximity was already taking them apart.}

{bex_relationship < 5: The cracks have been partially filled. Someone has been tracing the broken lines with a tool, deepening the grooves, extending the containment pattern across the fractures. Recent work. Careful work. But incomplete. Whoever was doing this couldn't finish.}

The portal breathes. The exhale hits. Heat, pressure, the column of copper and sulfur boiling up from the absence. You brace against the wall. Your lungs flatten. Your eyes flood. The force rolls through you and the amber light flares white at the edges and the stone burns under your hand.

Hold on.

The exhale passes. The pressure drops. The portal draws inward. One second. Maybe less than a second. The window between breaths.

You reach into your jacket pocket. The key. Heavy, real, the fob smooth under your thumb. A key to a car that doesn't exist anymore. The parking spot at the motel has been clean asphalt for two days. No oil stain. No tire marks. The car is gone the way everything goes in Veinrock: completely, as if it was never there.

The metal edge of the key is sufficient. Not for carving from scratch. For scoring existing stone, for deepening a groove that's already started, for completing a line that someone else began. You don't need to build the lock. You need to close it.

The last trace of your life outside this town. The last object that connects you to the highway, the drive, the apartment in Las Vegas, the person you were six days ago. You're going to drag it across stone until the circuit is complete.

The portal exhales again. The force. The heat. The sulfur. You press your back to the wall and hold.

-> sealing_ritual


= sealing_ritual

The job was simple. The job was always simple.

Window bangs. You scream. The guests flinch. The window shuts. Reset. Wait for the next group. Three minutes.

Event. Action. Reset.

The timing is in your body now. The sequence lives in the part of you that doesn't think, that just acts.

Window. Scream. Reset.

The ritual is the same rhythm.

The portal exhales. Event. You hold on, press yourself against the stone, let the force roll through you. Action. The pressure drops. The pause. A second. You move. You drop to your knees at the first fracture and press the key's edge into the groove. The metal bites stone. The line extends. One inch. Two.

The portal exhales.

You stop. Flatten against the wall. The heat spikes. The sulfur hits the back of your throat. Your lungs refuse to expand. The amber light flares so bright the chamber goes white.

* [Hold on.]

- It passes.

The pause. You move. Back to the fracture. The key against the stone. The groove deepens. The line extends, connecting the broken arc to the intact one on the far side. The containment pattern is in your hands. Not in your notebook. Not in language. In the movement itself. The sequence your fingers know. Your hand follows the curve without hesitation, the angle of intersection, the correct direction to close the arc. Muscle memory. The one thing that lives below what the mine can reach.

The portal exhales.

* [Hold on.]

{bex_relationship >= 5: The second fracture is the one Bex started. Their work is clean. The groove is deep. The angle precise. They got three-quarters of the way across before they couldn't anymore. Before the proximity took enough of them that their hands forgot the next line. You pick up where they stopped. The key fits into the groove Bex cut and you extend it the rest of the way. Your hand over theirs. Decades of maintenance, and you're the one who gets to finish it.}

{bex_relationship < 5: The second fracture is longer than the first. The partial fill helps. Someone's work, you don't know whose, gives you a starting point. You press the key into the groove and extend. The containment pattern guides your hand. Curve, intersect, close. The metal grates against the stone.}

The portal exhales. Stronger this time. The force knocks you sideways. Your shoulder hits the wall. The window shoulder, the one that's been locked since the first shift, since six hours in the boo hole taught your trapezius the shape of a pneumatic hinge's recoil. Pain spikes from shoulder to jaw. You hold on.

The pause is shorter. You can feel it compressing. The system straining toward capacity, the portal accelerating, the rhythm tightening. The biggest crowd of the week is above you, two hundred people screaming into the machine, and the machine is converting all of it into this: the exhale, the heat, the widening.

You're racing it. Complete the pattern before the portal finishes opening.

{erasure_level >= 7: Your hands know the pattern. The rest of you is less certain. The edges of your fingers are soft in the amber light, translucent, and when you press the key to the stone you can't always tell where your hand ends and the light begins. But the movement is correct. The angle is correct. The ritual knowledge lives below the level of what you are, in something older than memory, and your hands close the arc even as the rest of you thins. You are enough for this. You have to be enough for this.}

{erasure_level < 7: Two fractures closed. One left. Your fingers are scraped raw. Stone dust coats your hands. The key's edge is wearing blunt. You can feel the metal deforming with each stroke, the edge rounding, losing its bite. It doesn't need to last forever. It needs to last three more minutes.}

The portal exhales.

* [Hold on.]

- The pause.

* [Move.]

The third fracture. The longest one. It runs along the base of the wall where the stone shifted furthest, a crack wide enough to fit your fingertip into, following the curve of the chamber floor. You get down on your knees. The stone is hot under your legs. The amber light pulses from the portal beside you, close, so close you can feel the absence pulling at your skin.

The key against the stone. You start at the near end, where the intact carving terminates at the lip of the crack. The groove follows the containment pattern's curve. A long, slow arc connecting to the intact section on the far side. The farthest distance. The longest line. Your hand moves and the metal scores the stone and the line extends one inch across the fractured surface.

Window. Scream. Reset.

* [Keep going.]

- Inch by inch.

The portal exhales.

The strongest yet. The force lifts you off your knees and throws you against the base of the wall. Your ribs hit stone. The air goes out of you. The heat is physical, a weight on your back, pinning you to the floor. The sulfur is so thick you can't breathe through it. Your vision whites out.

Your fingers are still on the key. Your hand is still against the stone. The groove is under the key's edge, the last line, the longest crack, and you didn't let go.

The exhale passes. The pause. Shorter than the last. Half a second. Maybe less.

Your hand moves.

The key drags across the final inch of stone. The arc connects. The line meets the intact carving on the far side. The groove is shallow. The key is almost smooth now, the edge worn to nothing. But it's there. Scored into the rock. The circuit closes.

The containment pattern is complete.

Silence.

The amber light dims to the color of old honey and the pulsing stops. The air cools. Five degrees. Ten. The heat that rose from the portal in waves settles and dissipates and what's left is just air. Cave air. Cold and mineral and still.

The portal is still there. The circle in the floor where the stone ends and the absence begins. It hasn't closed. It hasn't filled in. But it's quiet. The breathing has stopped.

You sit on the floor of the chamber. The stone is cooling under you. Your hands are raw, stone dust in the cuts, the edgeless key still between your fingers.

You breathe. The air doesn't push back.

-> buried_ascent


= buried_ascent

The mine is just a mine.

You climb. Past the last of the carvings in the ancient stone and into the cut corridors, the mining shafts, the infrastructure of a business that operated here for decades before HorrorCo arrived.

The screaming reaches you somewhere around mid-level.

Guests. Above you, in the set-dressed corridors, the show is running. The screams filter down through the rock: distorted, layered, the sound of two hundred people being frightened on schedule.

They're just screams. Real, but not real in the way they were an hour ago. Not the fear frequency that fed the portal. Just people in a haunted attraction, doing what they paid to do. Getting scared. Laughing about it. Grabbing each other in the dark.

You climb past the utility corridor and back into the haunt. The fog rolls past your ankles. The speakers pulse bass. The amber light here is fake, nothing like the steady glow in the chamber below.

You're backstage. You're inside the machine. And the machine doesn't know anything changed.

-> buried_costume_room


= buried_costume_room

The costume room.

Bex's station. The work light is on.

The small articulated lamp clipped to the mirror's edge, the one Bex used for detail work to make a dead miner look dead. The bulb casts a warm circle on the mirror's surface.

Someone has traced a design across the glass. In greasepaint. The same circuit you just scored into the stone of the portal chamber. Drawn with the steady hand of someone who has worked this medium for decades.

A signature.

You stand in front of the mirror. You look tired.

You look like yourself.

* [Leave the lamp on.]
    -> buried_exit
* [Turn the lamp off.]
    -> buried_exit


= buried_exit

Every haunt has one. The final scare. The send-off. An actor with a chainsaw. chain removed, blade dull, pure noise and motion. Chasing the last group through the final corridor and out the exit door. Standard industry.

The chainsaw revs. The guests shriek. You follow behind them into the cold air and parking lot lights.

You just sealed an ancient portal with a car key. The machine doesn't know. The crowd doesn't know.

Nobody needs to know.

You walk across the parking lot. Past the cars. Past the queue. Past the building that hunches against the mountain, dark and loud and running at full power. The hum is gone from the ground. You can feel its absence in the soles of your feet. The gravel crunches under your shoes and the sound is just gravel.

-> buried_diner


= buried_diner

The diner is open. The sign glows in the window. The same sign that's been glowing every night you've walked in: white neon, one letter dimmer than the rest, the R in DINER flickering.

You push through the door.

Gloria is behind the counter. She looks up when you walk in.

One look.

Her face has changed.

"Sit."

She knows.

{has_gloria_medallion:
You reach inside your shirt. The chain. The disc. Martin Aldrich's medallion. You've worn it against your chest since this morning. It's warm from your skin.

You take it off. Set it on the counter.

Gloria looks at it. Her hand moves to it slowly. She picks it up. Holds it in her palm. The chain pools between her fingers.

"He wore this every shift," she says. Quiet. Not to you. To the medallion. To him. "Thirty years. Every shift."

She closes her hand around the medallion and puts it in her apron pocket.
}

Gloria pours two cups of coffee. She sits with you and wraps her hands around her mug.

* ["It's done."]
    You say it. Out loud. In the warm diner with the coffee and the neon and the quiet.
    Gloria nods once. She already knew. But hearing it matters. Saying it matters. The words land on the counter between you and sit there, solid, real.
    "I know," she says.
    -> buried_close

* [You don't have to say it.]
    You don't say anything. You drink your coffee. Gloria drinks hers. The silence between you is full of knowing.
    After a while, Gloria says, "The floor is quiet."
    You nod.
    "First time in a long time," she trails off.
    -> buried_close


= buried_close

Gloria brings you a meatloaf dinner.

"You look like you haven't eaten in a week. Eat."

The meatloaf is {ate_meatloaf: still} decent.

-> END
